Title:
catdumper — In-memory LSASS MiniDump + RC4/Base64 + HTTPS exfiltration

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
catdumper is a Windows LSASS credential dumping tool that snapshots the LSASS process and generates a MiniDump, then RC4-encrypts and Base64-encodes the dump entirely in memory. It exfiltrates the encrypted dump and its randomly generated key over HTTPS to a companion Python Flask server (exfil.py), which decodes/decrypts and writes the dump to disk for offline parsing (e.g., with Mimikatz). The project focuses on evasion: compile-time polymorphism to vary signatures, string obfuscation (e.g., “lsass.exe”), and randomized packet sizing/timing. It also attempts to bypass userland EDR hooks by unhooking NtReadVirtualMemory and performing additional benign API activity to reduce heuristic suspicion. Useful primarily for red teams/pentesters and malware researchers evaluating LSASS dumping tradecraft and common EDR bypass patterns.

Technical Focus:
- LSASS snapshotting and MiniDump generation (Windows API)
- In-memory encryption (RC4) and Base64 encoding
- HTTPS exfiltration to a Flask C2/receiver
- Userland unhooking of NtReadVirtualMemory (EDR hook evasion)
- Compile-time polymorphism / signature variation (RNG-based)
- String obfuscation and traffic randomization (timing/size)

Use Cases:
- Dumping LSASS credentials while minimizing on-disk artifacts
- Testing EDR/AV detections around MiniDumpWriteDump-style activity and API hooking
- Simulating credential theft + encrypted exfiltration in purple-team exercises
- Researching/benchmarking userland unhooking approaches and heuristic-evasion patterns

Keywords:
LSASS, MiniDump, MiniDumpWriteDump, process snapshot, NtReadVirtualMemory, unhooking, EDR evasion, userland hooks, RC4, Base64, in-memory, HTTPS exfiltration, Flask, Mimikatz, Windows API, string obfuscation, polymorphism, compile-time RNG, traffic randomization, credential dumping